        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(gateway): bound config file opener",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpening the configured file from the Control UI could wait indefinitely when a platform opener did not exit. A single hard timeout is not safe on Linux, however, because `xdg-open` may synchronously own a successfully opened foreground editor.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe opener lifecycle now follows each platform's contract:\n\n- macOS `open` and Windows `Start-Process` remain bounded by a five-second hard timeout because their launcher exit represents startup;\n- Linux `xdg-open` is detached with ignored stdio and `unref()`. The gateway observes its first five seconds to preserve immediate `ENOENT`, headless exit-code 3, and other launcher failures, then treats a still-running foreground handler as successfully launched without killing it.\n\nThe owner-local process behavior was extracted from the oversized gateway handler into `config-open.ts`; response, path-sanitization, command-selection, and PowerShell quoting behavior remain unchanged.\n\n## User Impact\n\nThe config-open RPC no longer waits forever. Linux users can keep long-running foreground editors opened by `xdg-open`, while missing-command and headless failures still return the existing actionable config path. macOS and Windows retain a bounded launcher wait.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Node 24.15.0: `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/gateway/server-methods/config-open.test.ts src/gateway/server-methods/config.test.ts` (2 files, 15/15 tests passed)\n- `oxfmt --check` on all four changed files\n- `oxlint` on all four changed files\n- `git diff --check`\n- Live `/usr/bin/xdg-open` no-handler probe exited with code 3 in about 20ms and was classified as handler unavailable\n- Controlled long-lived Linux handler proof: the helper returned after the five-second observation window, did not call `kill`, and the detached child later wrote its completion marker\n- Independent adversarial review: clean; Execa 9.6.1 cleanup/reject semantics and Node detached/unref behavior were checked directly\n\nProof gap: no native macOS or Windows opener scenario was run; those paths retain the previously tested shared-runner timeout behavior.\n\nAI-assisted: yes. The implementation and focused lifecycle paths were independently reviewed before the follow-up commit.\n",
